# Surveyor-unembed sweep — pre-registration and result
# Date: 2026-09-02 (red/green pair for test_phys_alloc remain 2026-09-01)

## Pre-registration (written BEFORE the run)

Baseline: sweep run 2 (2026-09-01, /tmp/make_test_sweep2.log) was
1028/1045 across 28 "^Passed:" lines, make exit 1. The only failing
suite was test-survey at 21/38 (17 failures), all one mechanism:
PARTITION-MAP printed "AHCI not init" because the harness ran
AHCI-INIT against the embedded AHCI before S" SURVEYOR" LOAD-VOCAB
re-loaded AHCI from blocks — SURVEYOR compiled against the fresh
copy, whose init state was zero.

Fix under test: reorder only — LOAD-VOCAB first, then USING AHCI /
AHCI-INIT / USING SURVEYOR (test_install.py's proven order). No
kernel, vocab, or Makefile change.

Prediction: if the reorder is the whole fix, the next full sweep is
  1045/1045 across 28 "^Passed:" lines, exit 0.
Anything else is a finding.

## Product defect recorded (deferred list, not fixed here)

LOAD-VOCAB resolves REQUIRES with no already-loaded check
(catalog-resolver.fth LOAD-VOCAB-INNER: LOADING-PUSH guards only
the current resolution pass). On a build where a dependency is
already embedded, it compiles a SECOND copy of that vocabulary;
find_ walks to the newer one, nothing errors, and "initialized"
silently becomes a property of which copy you last spoke to.
Two-representations-of-one-fact at the vocabulary level. This bites
operators, not just harnesses.

The G6 runbook §3a is safe BY ACCIDENT: it uses THRU of catalog
ranges, which loads the named blocks only and never re-resolves
dependencies. That is protection nobody chose — recorded here so
the eventual fix (an already-loaded check in LOAD-VOCAB-INNER, or
an explicit ruling that re-load-and-shadow is the intended
semantics) is made deliberately.

